00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:18.790
it was a potential risk.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.160
And then as time went on, we
saw that happen less and less,

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:22.780
especially with proper training.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:24.280
- [Narrator] Dylan and Jessica consume

00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:27.400
nearly twice the recommended
calories for their age.

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:30.180
- While we do give
recommendations on caloric intake,

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:32.340
that is a wide ranging recommendation

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:33.560
that needs to be individualized.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.060
So for children that are
highly active like them,

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.240
and we see how their weight
and their BMI is trending,

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.570
we can make different
changes to their diets

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:43.030
and to their recommendations.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:44.630
So the fact that they're eating a lot more

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:47.260
than an average child
isn't that wild to me

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:49.010
because they're burning a lot of calories

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:51.560
by doing all of these
workouts several times a day.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:55.380
- Hi, I'm Kelly Athena

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:58.853
and I'm obsessed with
rescuing food from dumpsters.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:01.693
- I'm with it.

00:11:01.693 --> 00:11:03.770
We waste so much food in this country

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:05.040
while other countries starve,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:06.390
I don't think this is a terrible idea,

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:07.700
I just hope they're doing it safely.

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:09.380
- [Narrator] The average
American household

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.340
spends over $500 a month on groceries.

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:14.180
- And we oftentimes
spend money on groceries

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:16.530
that look the prettiest and waste the food

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.860
that may not look pretty
but is just as nutritious,

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:19.800
just as tasty.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:23.433
- Everything I have in my
fridge, I got from the dumpster.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.630
Apples, cucumbers,

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:28.320
pickles, condiments,

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.670
grapes, cantaloupe.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:31.630
Here's some soy milk I had,

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:34.710
and it even expired more than a month ago.

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.680
- Sometimes it's a full
on expiration date,

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.790
but you really need to
understand what that date is for

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:41.600
'cause that date could be
like for ideal freshness

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.470
or for ideal taste, it still might be

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.760
calorically, nutritionally, valuable.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.630
- [Kelly] I'm not afraid to
eat right out of the dumpster

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.540
if it's good stuff.

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:50.890
There's this peach,

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.550
if I peel it, that'll
be pretty safe to eat.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:55.360
If I eat out at a restaurant,

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.980
I don't know who's preparing
that food bag there.

00:11:57.980 --> 00:11:59.480
- I don't know if I'm a fan of that logic.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:00.750
You don't know who's preparing the food?

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:01.770
At least it's cooked.

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:04.590
Here, you're getting food that's
been living in a dumpster.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.110
What if a raccoon pooped on it?

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:06.943
- [Narrator] She is spicing up

00:12:06.943 --> 00:12:09.860
the four month old
dumpster pizza with cheese

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.380
two weeks past its expiration date,

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:15.220
overripe tomatoes Kelly found a week ago.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.500
- [Husband] Maybe a little diced fennel.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:19.580
- [Narrator] Fennel found
at the bottom of a trash bin

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:20.640
just this morning.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.490
- Well, it's a fresh dumpster fennel.

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:23.720
- [Narrator] And frozen chicken.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:26.650
- Chicken, if it was even
not frozen for a short period

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:28.780
of time, bacteria can
start proliferating on it,

00:12:28.780 --> 00:12:31.420
especially if it wasn't
stored or prepared properly.

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:32.253
Oh, man.

00:12:32.253 --> 00:12:33.570
I've had a personal close friend of mine

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:36.860
who eats thoroughly cooked
foods, eat out at a restaurant,

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.860
get an infection from one of those foods

00:12:38.860 --> 00:12:41.430
and then develop an abscess,
which is a pocket of infection

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.880
inside his brain, and he
still has neurologic effects

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:44.730
as a result of that,

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.030
so this isn't like totally benign.

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:49.070
I also reviewed Family by the Ton,

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:51.410
living life as an 800 pound person.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.660
Click here to check that out.

